		<volgnummer></volgnummer>
		<niveau>2</niveau>
		<onderwerp>Object-Oriented Programming</onderwerp>
		<header>
			<regel>Which principle of object-oriented programming promotes the reusability of code?</regel>
		</header>
		<keuzes>
			<A>DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself)</A>
			<B>Encapsulation</B>
			<C>Inheritance</C>
			<D>Polymorphism</D>
		</keuzes>
		<antwoord>A</antwoord>
		<userantwoord></userantwoord>
		<show>none</show>
		<uitleg>The "DRY" (Don't Repeat Yourself) principle of object-oriented programming promotes the reusability of code. It encourages developers to avoid duplicating code by abstracting common functionality into reusable components or functions. Encapsulation is a principle that focuses on bundling data and methods together, inheritance allows objects to inherit properties and methods from other objects, and polymorphism allows objects to take multiple forms and respond to the same method differently.</uitleg>
	</vraag>
	<vraag>
		<disabled></disabled>
		<guid>a0017f79-1b8f-4682-8675-976d77d32fd4</guid>
		<volgnummer></volgnummer>
		<niveau>2</niveau>
		<onderwerp>Version Control Systems</onderwerp>
		<header>
			<regel>Which version control system is distributed and allows for offline work and local branching?</regel>
		</header>
		<keuzes>
			<A>Git</A>
			<B>Subversion (SVN)</B>
			<C>Mercurial</C>
			<D>Perforce</D>
		</keuzes>
		<antwoord>A</antwoord>
		<userantwoord></userantwoord>
		<show>none</show>
		<uitleg>Git is a distributed version control system that allows for offline work and local branching. It provides a complete copy of the repository on each user's machine, allowing them to work independently and synchronize changes later. Subversion (SVN), Mercurial, and Perforce are also version control systems, but they have different characteristics and workflows compared to Git.</uitleg>
